WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.190
Dropout does this seemingly crazy thing of randomly knocking out units in your network.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:08.470
Why does it work so well as a regularizer?

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.930
Let's gain some better intuition.

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:16.030
In the previous video, I gave this intuition that Dropout randomly knocks out units in

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:21.030
your network so it's as if on every iteration you're working with a smaller neural network.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:26.630
And so using a smaller neural network seems like it should have a regularizing effect.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:31.230
Here's a second intuition, which is, you know, let's look at it from the perspective of a

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.910
single unit.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:34.910
Let's say this one.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:40.190
Now for this unit to do its job, it has four inputs and it needs to generate some meaningful

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.470
output.

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:46.390
Now with Dropout, the inputs can get randomly eliminated.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:50.810
Sometimes those two units will get eliminated, sometimes a different unit will get eliminated.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:55.190
So what this means is that this unit, which I'm circling in purple, it can't rely on

00:00:55.190 --> 00:01:01.710
any one feature because any one feature could go away at random or any one of its own inputs

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.070
could go away at random.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:09.870
So in particular, it would be reluctant to put all of its bets on say just this input,

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:10.870
right?

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:15.070
The weights will be reluctant to put too much weight on any one input because it could

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.230
go away.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:20.710
So this unit will be more motivated to spread out its weights and give you a little bit

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:26.390
of weight to each of the four inputs to this unit.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:32.230
And by spreading out the weights, this will tend to have an effect of shrinking the squared

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.270
norm of the weights.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:41.150
And so similar to what we saw with L2 regularization, the effect of implementing Dropout is that it

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:46.390
strengths the weights and does similar to L2 regularization that helps to prevent overfitting.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:52.310
But it turns out that Dropout can formally be shown to be an adaptive form of L2 regularization,

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:57.350
but the L2 penalty on different weights are different depending on the size of the activations

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.990
being multiplied into that weight.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:06.990
But to summarize, it is possible to show that Dropout has a similar effect to L2 regularization.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.830
Only the L2 regularization applied to different weights can be a little bit different and

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.690
even more adaptive to the scale of different inputs.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.110
One more detail for when you're implementing Dropout.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.630
Here's a network where you have three input features.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:24.790
This is seven hidden units here, seven, three, two, one.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:29.670
So one of the parameters we had to choose was the Keep Prop, which is a chance of keeping

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:31.670
a unit in each layer.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:36.710
So it is also feasible to vary Keep Prop by layer.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:42.590
So for the first layer, your matrix W1 will be 7 by 3.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:46.070
Your second weight matrix will be 7 by 7.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:50.510
W3 will be 3 by 7, and so on.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.950
And so W2 is actually the biggest weight matrix, right?

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:58.870
Because they're actually the largest set of parameters being W2, which is 7 by 7.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:04.470
So to prevent, to reduce overfitting of that matrix, maybe for this layer, I guess

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:11.350
this is layer 2, you might have a Keep Prop that's relatively low, say 0.5.

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.350
Whereas for different layers where you might worry less about overfitting,

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:21.030
you could have a higher Keep Prop, maybe just 0.7, maybe this is 0.7.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.230
And then for layers where you don't worry about overfitting at all,

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.990
you can have a Keep Prop of 1.0.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:31.270
So for clarity, these are numbers I'm drawing in the purple boxes.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:35.150
These could be different Keep Props for different layers.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:38.950
Notice that the Keep Prop of 1.0 means that you're keeping every unit,

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:42.950
and so you're really not using brought dropout for that layer.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:45.910
But for layers where you're more worried about overfitting, really the layers

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.230
with a lot of parameters, you could set Keep Prop to be smaller to apply

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.150
a more powerful form of dropout.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.070
It's kind of like cranking up the regularization parameter,

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:59.870
Lambda for L2 regularization, where you try to regularize some layers more than others.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.350
And technically, you can also apply dropout to the input layer,

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:04.950
where you can have some chance of, you know,

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.790
just axing out one or more of the input features.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:12.070
Although in practice, usually don't do that that often.

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.750
And so a Keep Prop of 1.0 is quite common for the input layer.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.510
You might also use a very high value, maybe 0.9.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.830
But it's much less likely that, you know,

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:23.230
you want to eliminate half of the input features.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.150
So usually, a Keep Prop, if you apply it at all,

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:31.270
will be a number close to one if you even apply dropout at all

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.590
to the input layer.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:35.670
So just to summarize, if you're more worried about some layers

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.110
overfitting than others, you can set a lower Keep Prop

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.790
for some layers than others.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.310
The downside is this gives you even more hyperparameters

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:45.430
to search for using cross-validation.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:49.430
One other alternative might be to have some layers where you apply dropout

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:51.230
and some layers where you don't apply dropout

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.270
and then just have one hyperparameter,

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.430
which is a Keep Prop for the layers for which you do apply dropout.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:59.750
And before we wrap up, just a couple of implantational tips.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.430
Many of the first successful implementations of dropout

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.510
were to computer vision.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:07.390
So in computer vision, the input size is so big.

00:05:07.390 --> 00:05:09.430
You're inputting all these pixels

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:11.830
that you almost never have enough data.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:15.430
And so dropout is very frequently used by in computer vision.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.150
And there are some computer vision researchers

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.550
that pretty much always use it almost as a default.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.070
But really, the thing to remember

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.310
is that dropout is a regularization technique.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:27.590
It helps prevent overfitting.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:31.870
And so unless my algorithm is overfitting,

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:33.990
I wouldn't actually bother to use dropout.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:37.470
So it's used somewhat less often in other application areas.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.030
It's just for computer vision.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:41.110
You usually just don't have enough data,

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:42.630
so you're almost always overfitting,

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.750
which is why there tend to be some computer vision researchers

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:46.950
that are just squared by dropout.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:51.110
But the intuition doesn't always generalize, I think,

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.150
to other disciplines.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:58.790
One big downside of dropout is that the cost function j

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:01.110
is no longer well-defined.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:04.430
On every iteration, you're randomly

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.390
curling off a bunch of nodes.

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:10.790
And so if you're double-checking the performance of gradient

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:15.110
descent, it's actually harder to double-check that you have

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:19.310
a well-defined cost function j that is going downhill

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:21.190
on every iteration.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.550
Because the cost function j that you're optimizing

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:26.230
is actually less well-defined, or is it

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:28.070
certainly hard to calculate.

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.790
So you lose this debugging tool to either plot

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.510
a draft like this.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:35.510
So what I usually do is turn off dropout.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:38.590
You will set keyprop equals 1, and then run my code

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:41.550
and make sure that it is monotonically decreasing j.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.430
And then turn on dropout and hope

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:47.550
that I didn't introduce the bulk into my code during dropout.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.670
Because you need other ways, I guess,

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.910
but not plotting these figures to make sure

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:54.470
that your code is working, the gradient descent is working,

00:06:54.470 --> 00:06:56.630
even with dropout.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:00.590
So with that, there are still a few more regularization

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:02.470
techniques that we're feeling knowing.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:06.350
Let's talk about a few more such techniques in the next video.
